virtio-net: send from guest memory, on the vCPU, or to an in-process stack

- The unixgram backend sends each frame with sendmsg from the guest's
  buffers instead of copying it into a staging buffer first.
- A vCPU that kicks the transmit queue sends frames of up to 4 KiB itself
  (VirtioDevice::notify_inline), sparing a wakeup of the net worker on
  every request; larger frames stay with the worker, which keeps the
  guest producing while it sends. The transmit queue is shared under a
  mutex the vCPU only try-locks.
- A new backend, InProcess (net/inproc.rs), passes frames by function
  call to a network stack loaded into the VMM process: the guest's frames
  go to the stack's transmit function, and the stack writes frames for
  the guest into the receive queue on its own threads, with a bounded
  backlog for when the guest has no buffers. NetDevice::new_in_process
  creates it.

